        "explain-survey": {
            "kind": "exp-lookit-exit-survey",
            "debriefing": {
                "title": "Thank you for participating in this study. This work would not be possible without you!",
                "blocks": [
                    {
                        "title": "What did my child do today?\n",
                        "text": "The sounds around us are often diverse and sometimes meaningful. Sometimes these sounds are speech; other times these sounds are water running during bath time. Children can learn important information from the sounds in their environments, even when these sounds are not speech. These sounds can sometimes affect how well we accomplish goals. In this study, we were interested in whether preschool children understand that background noise can affect their cognitive performance. Your child played two cognitive games and then predicted their performance on each. Importantly, children played one of the games while background noise played. We expect that if children understand that background noise can affect performance, they should believe their performance was better when there was no background noise. \n \nThis study will help us understand how children use their environment for learning, even without explicit teaching. We were interested in what your child thought, so there were no right or wrong answers. Lots of other children have also participated in this study. Sometimes they responded a little similarly to your child and sometimes they responded a little differently. All answers are okay! \n \n If you want to learn more about how children think about the effects if noise, check out this article called <a href=https://escholarship.org/uc/item/1zd1m43h' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Young children’s reasoning about the epistemic consequences of auditory noise</a>."
                    },
                    {
                        "title": "How do I receive compensation?",
                        "text": "To thank you for your participation, we'll be emailing you a $5 Amazon gift card - this should arrive in your inbox within the next week after we confirm your consent video and check that your child is in the age range for this study (If you don't hear from us by then, feel free to reach out!). If you participate again with another child in the age range, you'll receive one gift card per child. Please note that we cannot compensate participation from children outside of the age range."
                    },
                    {
                        "title": "What if I have more questions?",
                        "text": "For any questions or concerns about your child's participation in this study, please contact Rondeline Williams at rondeline.williams@stanford.edu."
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
